WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.253
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Privileged Access learning path:

2
00:00:04.253 --> 00:00:06.912
PIM for Microsoft Entra roles as code.

3
00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:13.296
This module serves the project plan's
task A1.6 in Phase 1, A2.1 and A2.2

4
00:00:13.296 --> 00:00:19.912
in Phase 2, and A3.1 in Phase 3, with
Chapter 04 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:25.211
Each Microsoft Entra role has its own PIM
settings: how long an activation lasts,

6
00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:27.539
whether it needs approval, a ticket,

7
00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:31.713
an authentication context, and
how long an assignment may last.

8
00:00:31.713 --> 00:00:34.812
Set by hand in the portal,
they drift role by role.

9
00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:39.037
In this module you hold them as
code, one reviewed file per role.

10
00:00:39.037 --> 00:00:42.212
You see exactly what a change
will do before it happens,

11
00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:46.233
apply it to one rollout wave with
the least privilege the writes need,

12
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:49.901
and prove from a read-back that
the tenant now matches the files.

13
00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:54.610
The code is illustrative: tested
against fixtures and a fake.

14
00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.510
Why does this matter?

15
00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.850
Because this module writes the settings
that cause the documented lockout,

16
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.572
so it refuses both halves of it.

17
00:01:05.572 --> 00:01:10.023
Rule pim oh one fails a role file that
requires approval with no approver.

18
00:01:10.023 --> 00:01:14.432
Rule pim oh two fails the run if
fewer emergency accounts hold Global

19
00:01:14.432 --> 00:01:19.923
Administrator active, permanent and direct
than the emergency access file requires.

20
00:01:19.923 --> 00:01:24.673
The code changes no role setting until
Module 2's half holds in the tenant.

21
00:01:24.673 --> 00:01:27.591
And the Activation Gate
Checked switch is a person's

22
00:01:27.591 --> 00:01:30.373
statement that the activation
gate's policy is On.

23
00:01:30.373 --> 00:01:33.867
The apply won't set an
authentication context without it,

24
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:37.659
because the gate's state can't be
read from version one point oh.

25
00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:39.573
Any finding blocks the apply.

26
00:01:39.573 --> 00:01:44.020
And PIM itself won't remove the
last active Global Administrator.

27
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:50.610
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.257
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:53.257 --> 00:01:58.332
You'll explain the documented lockout's two
halves, and the check that refuses each.

30
00:01:58.332 --> 00:02:02.167
You'll name the seven activation and
assignment rules the code manages,

31
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:03.921
and where their bounds come from.

32
00:02:03.921 --> 00:02:08.520
You'll read a role's diff and the
plan's findings, pim oh one to oh nine.

33
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.503
You'll explain what the
read-only alert check,

34
00:02:11.503 --> 00:02:15.533
pim ten to thirteen, looks for,
and where each finding is fixed.

35
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:20.057
You'll explain the rollout waves, and why
a later wave waits for an earlier one.

36
00:02:20.057 --> 00:02:23.582
And you'll explain the order for
converting a standing assignment.

37
00:02:23.582 --> 00:02:28.580
Before you start, finish Module 2,
for the emergency accounts this module

38
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.994
refuses to proceed without, and Module 3,
for the activation gate and its policy.

39
00:02:33.994 --> 00:02:39.808
Lab 2 needs a worldwide or G C C test
tenant, never production, with Microsoft

40
00:02:39.808 --> 00:02:45.600
Entra I D P2 or I D Governance licenses
for the test users and approvers.

41
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.725
Unit 1: settings, rules and the lockout.

42
00:02:52.725 --> 00:02:56.425
Settings are per role, and
Microsoft Graph calls them rules.

43
00:02:56.425 --> 00:02:59.726
Microsoft Learn's mapping
guide for rules in PIM shows

44
00:02:59.726 --> 00:03:03.100
that each role's policy has
seventeen predefined rules.

45
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.294
By the program's design, the
code manages seven of them,

46
00:03:06.294 --> 00:03:11.012
the activation and assignment rules,
and leaves the notification rules alone.

47
00:03:11.012 --> 00:03:14.836
For the person who activates:
how long the activation lasts,

48
00:03:14.836 --> 00:03:19.674
what it requires, such as a ticket, the
authentication context, and approval.

49
00:03:19.674 --> 00:03:24.246
For the administrator who assigns: how
long an eligible assignment may last,

50
00:03:24.246 --> 00:03:28.962
how long an active assignment may last,
and what an active assignment requires.

51
00:03:28.962 --> 00:03:34.325
The authentication context needs its
policy first; that's Module 3's gate.

52
00:03:34.325 --> 00:03:35.763
And remember the lockout.

53
00:03:35.763 --> 00:03:39.963
Microsoft Learn's PIM settings page
says that with no approvers selected,

54
00:03:39.963 --> 00:03:43.544
the active Privileged Role and
Global Administrators become

55
00:03:43.544 --> 00:03:46.712
the default approvers, and in
the lockout there are none.

56
00:03:46.712 --> 00:03:51.980
Learn's fix has two halves: emergency
access accounts, and named approvers.

57
00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:00.480
Unit 2: the files, the checks, the
alert check and the conversion.

58
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.530
The role files are twenty-two, one per
role, with approvers named by alias.

59
00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.531
Beside them, a tiers file holds
Microsoft Learn's bounds and the

60
00:04:09.531 --> 00:04:13.993
program's caps for each tier, which
are this workbook's design guidance.

61
00:04:13.993 --> 00:04:19.104
Learn's pages disagree on how long an
activation may last: the role settings

62
00:04:19.104 --> 00:04:23.804
page allows up to twenty-four hours,
the PIM A P I overview at most eight.

63
00:04:23.804 --> 00:04:26.555
The code follows the
stricter, eight hours.

64
00:04:26.555 --> 00:04:30.617
Active assignments are capped at
fifteen days, the owner's decision.

65
00:04:30.617 --> 00:04:32.481
And the plan's order is fixed.

66
00:04:32.481 --> 00:04:35.763
The owner chose Tier 1 as the pilot class,

67
00:04:35.763 --> 00:04:39.842
so wave one is Tier 1, then
Tier 0, Tier 2 and read-only.

68
00:04:39.842 --> 00:04:43.718
The order after the first wave is
this workbook's design guidance.

69
00:04:43.718 --> 00:04:49.260
Rule pim oh seven refuses a wave until
every earlier wave matches its files.

70
00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.535
The checks, pim oh one to oh nine.

71
00:04:55.535 --> 00:04:58.162
Oh one and oh two are
the lockout's two halves,

72
00:04:58.162 --> 00:05:02.249
from Microsoft Learn's PIM settings
page: approval with no approver,

73
00:05:02.249 --> 00:05:06.847
and too few emergency accounts holding
Global Administrator permanently.

74
00:05:06.847 --> 00:05:10.704
Oh three is the program's design:
an approver group that's also

75
00:05:10.704 --> 00:05:14.560
the role's eligible group, so
approval becomes self-approval.

76
00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.872
Oh four is a file outside Microsoft
Learn's bounds or its tier's cap.

77
00:05:18.872 --> 00:05:24.461
Oh five warns on an authentication context,
because its policy must be On first.

78
00:05:24.461 --> 00:05:27.473
Oh six is a role with no
policy at tenant scope.

79
00:05:27.473 --> 00:05:29.423
Oh seven is the wave order.

80
00:05:29.423 --> 00:05:33.287
Oh eight is eligibility
outside the role's P R V group,

81
00:05:33.287 --> 00:05:36.522
which the access review
in Module 7 removes.

82
00:05:36.522 --> 00:05:39.530
And oh nine is a standing
active assignment:

83
00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.122
a user to convert, or a
service principal to document.

84
00:05:43.122 --> 00:05:47.488
Reads and writes are different tasks,
and the code follows the Graph A P I

85
00:05:47.488 --> 00:05:52.520
reference pages, which name narrower
permissions than Learn's how-to guides.

86
00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:57.383
The alert check.

87
00:05:57.383 --> 00:06:00.884
CISA's baseline under B
O D twenty-five oh-one

88
00:06:00.884 --> 00:06:04.195
asks for two alerts the
role files don't set.

89
00:06:04.195 --> 00:06:08.257
M S A A D seven point seven:
an alert on every eligible

90
00:06:08.257 --> 00:06:12.995
or active assignment to the
eight highly privileged roles.

91
00:06:12.995 --> 00:06:17.883
And seven point eight: an alert on
every Global Administrator activation.

92
00:06:17.883 --> 00:06:21.770
So a separate script reads them
under its own read-only task,

93
00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:23.833
pim alerts, and changes nothing.

94
00:06:23.833 --> 00:06:27.883
Pim ten and pim eleven check each
role's notification settings.

95
00:06:27.883 --> 00:06:30.941
Pim twelve and pim thirteen
check the PIM for Groups

96
00:06:30.941 --> 00:06:34.283
member policy of each group
that holds one of those roles.

97
00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:39.688
Each is a finding until an additional
recipient, the security monitoring mailbox,

98
00:06:39.688 --> 00:06:43.107
is set: under the role's role
settings, notifications,

99
00:06:43.107 --> 00:06:47.082
or the group's PIM for Groups
settings, member, notifications.

100
00:06:47.082 --> 00:06:49.370
PIM's default recipients don't count.

101
00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.808
On the fictional tenant,
all eleven are findings.

102
00:06:52.808 --> 00:06:57.120
Its tests are a separate suite,
in the baseline tests folder.

103
00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:04.199
Converting standing assignments
goes in a fixed order,

104
00:07:04.199 --> 00:07:06.008
and each row carries its state.

105
00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:11.158
Waiting For Eligibility: the role's
P R V group is made eligible first.

106
00:07:11.158 --> 00:07:16.132
Waiting For Confirmed Activation: the person
activates the role through the group,

107
00:07:16.132 --> 00:07:18.871
and the activation is
confirmed, with a ticket.

108
00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:22.745
Confirmed: only now can the
active assignment be removed.

109
00:07:22.745 --> 00:07:26.659
Only the Apply switch with the
Convert switch removes anything,

110
00:07:26.659 --> 00:07:31.608
and only for confirmed rows; the
confirmed-activations file carries each ticket.

111
00:07:31.608 --> 00:07:35.108
So no one loses a role before
they've shown they can activate it.

112
00:07:35.108 --> 00:07:40.140
And as a backstop, PIM won't remove
the last active Global Administrator.

113
00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:44.615
Try it.

114
00:07:44.615 --> 00:07:47.356
Lab 1 runs in one PowerShell 7 session,

115
00:07:47.356 --> 00:07:51.865
because steps four to six use
the settings step three defines.

116
00:07:51.865 --> 00:07:56.227
Step one sends Microsoft Learn's
samples to the fake, as published.

117
00:07:56.227 --> 00:07:57.802
Thirteen tests pass.

118
00:07:57.802 --> 00:08:01.064
The fourth shows that all
six of Learn's updates apply,

119
00:08:01.064 --> 00:08:06.253
and yet Application Administrator still
differs from its file in six of seven rules.

120
00:08:06.253 --> 00:08:08.715
Learn's samples aren't
the program's settings.

121
00:08:08.715 --> 00:08:11.415
Step two lists each task's permissions.

122
00:08:11.415 --> 00:08:14.883
Planning needs three reads:
role assignment schedules,

123
00:08:14.883 --> 00:08:18.577
role eligibility schedules
and role management policies.

124
00:08:18.577 --> 00:08:24.389
Applying adds two writes: role eligibility
schedules and role management policies.

125
00:08:24.389 --> 00:08:28.528
And converting adds only one
write: role assignment schedules.

126
00:08:28.528 --> 00:08:31.560
The output on the slide is wrapped to fit.

127
00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:39.535
Step three dry-runs wave one,
the pilot class, Tier 1.

128
00:08:39.535 --> 00:08:44.214
First you put the run's settings in
one variable, the lab table: the cloud,

129
00:08:44.214 --> 00:08:47.925
the fixture tenant, the bindings
from aliases to groups,

130
00:08:47.925 --> 00:08:53.410
the emergency access file, the exceptions,
the confirmed activations, and the date.

131
00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:54.960
Later steps reuse it.

132
00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.373
Then run wave one.

133
00:08:56.373 --> 00:08:59.786
Nine roles, no findings,
thirteen warnings.

134
00:08:59.786 --> 00:09:04.460
The plan: forty-three rule updates,
eight eligibility requests, and two

135
00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:09.860
standing assignments to convert, none
confirmed yet, so neither would be removed.

136
00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:11.960
It's a dry run, so nothing was sent,

137
00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:17.420
and the manifest lists every request it
would send and the hash of every file.

138
00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:25.345
Step four reads the diff for one
role, Exchange Administrator.

139
00:09:25.345 --> 00:09:28.695
It was partly set by hand,
so its diff is short.

140
00:09:28.695 --> 00:09:31.545
The authentication context goes to C one.

141
00:09:31.545 --> 00:09:36.256
Approval goes to required, single
stage, with justification,

142
00:09:36.256 --> 00:09:41.533
one day to decide, and the approvers
named: the group P I M Approvers T1.

143
00:09:41.533 --> 00:09:46.083
And the active assignment maximum goes
from a hundred eighty days to fifteen.

144
00:09:46.083 --> 00:09:49.711
Then the findings, leaving
out the nine pim oh five rows,

145
00:09:49.711 --> 00:09:52.694
which ask you to confirm
the activation gate.

146
00:09:52.694 --> 00:09:55.448
Three are pim oh nine,
standing assignments:

147
00:09:55.448 --> 00:10:00.648
Erin Patel on Application Administrator
and Carl Ops on Exchange Administrator,

148
00:10:00.648 --> 00:10:05.389
both to convert through the P R V group,
and an automation service principal

149
00:10:05.389 --> 00:10:10.207
to document as an exception or replace,
because this code removes users only.

150
00:10:10.207 --> 00:10:15.539
And one is pim oh eight: Dana Reyes
is eligible outside the P R V group,

151
00:10:15.539 --> 00:10:19.707
which Module 7's access review
removes, not this code.

152
00:10:19.707 --> 00:10:21.920
The slide trims the output.

153
00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:30.370
Step five tries wave two, Tier
0, before wave one is done.

154
00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:34.940
Five roles, nine findings, five
warnings and two information items,

155
00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.845
and the run is blocked: fix
every finding before the apply.

156
00:10:38.845 --> 00:10:41.213
The nine findings are pim oh seven,

157
00:10:41.213 --> 00:10:44.933
one for each Tier 1 role that
doesn't match its file yet.

158
00:10:44.933 --> 00:10:49.182
The two Info rows are the emergency
accounts, kept permanent active.

159
00:10:49.182 --> 00:10:51.807
Step six applies wave one to the fake.

160
00:10:51.807 --> 00:10:56.958
The first command stops before any write:
the plan sets an authentication context,

161
00:10:56.958 --> 00:11:02.108
so confirm its Conditional Access policy
is On, then pass Activation Gate Checked.

162
00:11:02.108 --> 00:11:03.907
The second command passes it.

163
00:11:03.907 --> 00:11:08.512
It sends the rule updates first and
the eligibility requests second,

164
00:11:08.512 --> 00:11:13.358
fifty-one requests, then reads back:
zero differences from the role files.

165
00:11:13.358 --> 00:11:16.790
Step seven runs the tests,
three hundred thirty pass,

166
00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:19.300
and you delete the output folder.

167
00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:29.112
Lab 2 works one role in your own test
tenant, worldwide or G C C, never production,

168
00:11:29.112 --> 00:11:32.522
with Microsoft Entra I
D P2 or I D Governance

169
00:11:32.522 --> 00:11:35.837
licenses for the test users and approvers.

170
00:11:35.837 --> 00:11:37.747
Use a test copy of the files,

171
00:11:37.747 --> 00:11:41.825
and the emergency access file
you wrote in Module 2's Lab 2.

172
00:11:41.825 --> 00:11:46.006
Check the prerequisites first:
Module 2's check with no finding,

173
00:11:46.006 --> 00:11:48.338
and the activation gate's policy On.

174
00:11:48.338 --> 00:11:50.686
Then make the lab's role and groups,

175
00:11:50.686 --> 00:11:53.874
dry-run as a reader app,
and apply as a writer app.

176
00:11:53.874 --> 00:11:58.325
Then activate as a second test user,
and convert their standing assignment.

177
00:11:58.325 --> 00:12:02.920
The reader gets the three plan permissions;
the writer adds the two apply writes;

178
00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:07.237
and the role assignment schedule write
is granted only for the conversion.

179
00:12:07.237 --> 00:12:11.171
To back out, restore the rules
from the apply run's before file,

180
00:12:11.171 --> 00:12:14.712
remove what the apply added,
and delete the lab folders.

181
00:12:14.712 --> 00:12:17.230
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

182
00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:22.367
The federal requirement.

183
00:12:22.367 --> 00:12:26.717
The role files, the plan
and the read-back are the

184
00:12:26.717 --> 00:12:30.731
evidence for the plan's
tasks A1.6, A2.1 and A2.2.

185
00:12:30.731 --> 00:12:36.688
From NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three they support A C six,

186
00:12:36.688 --> 00:12:42.180
least privilege in time; A C two and
A C three, approval before activation;

187
00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:46.555
A C five, separation of duties;
and A C six, enhancement five.

188
00:12:46.555 --> 00:12:51.541
And from CISA's B O D twenty-five
oh-one and its ScubaGear baseline:

189
00:12:51.541 --> 00:12:57.543
M S A A D seven point four, no standing
assignment; seven point five, provisioning

190
00:12:57.543 --> 00:13:03.730
only through PIM; and seven point six,
approval to activate Global Administrator.

191
00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:07.483
The alert check's findings support
A U six, audit record review,

192
00:13:07.483 --> 00:13:10.132
and seven point seven
and seven point eight,

193
00:13:10.132 --> 00:13:15.430
the alerts on highly privileged assignments
and on Global Administrator activation.

194
00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:18.419
C M three and C M four, enhancement two,

195
00:13:18.419 --> 00:13:22.505
cover the change: reviewed
before, verified after.

196
00:13:22.505 --> 00:13:28.017
The evidence an ISSO keeps: the role files
and the tiers file; each wave's plan,

197
00:13:28.017 --> 00:13:32.551
diff and before files; the apply's
verify file and manifest; and,

198
00:13:32.551 --> 00:13:37.890
for each conversion, the confirmed
activations with their tickets.

199
00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.615
Check yourself with two questions.

200
00:13:43.615 --> 00:13:47.765
First: a role file requires
approval and names no approver.

201
00:13:47.765 --> 00:13:48.777
What happens?

202
00:13:48.777 --> 00:13:52.528
The answer is b: rule pim
oh one fails the file.

203
00:13:52.528 --> 00:13:55.703
It's half of the lockout
Microsoft Learn documents.

204
00:13:55.703 --> 00:13:59.778
Second: why does the apply need
the Activation Gate Checked switch?

205
00:13:59.778 --> 00:14:04.935
The answer is b: the gate's policy state
can't be read from version one point oh, so

206
00:14:04.935 --> 00:14:10.253
a person states that the activation gate's
policy is On before the context is set.

207
00:14:10.253 --> 00:14:15.115
You saw the first apply in Lab 1,
step six, stop before any write.

208
00:14:15.115 --> 00:14:20.390
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

209
00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:24.965
To sum up.

210
00:14:24.965 --> 00:14:28.537
You dry-ran the pilot wave, read
a role's diff rule by rule,

211
00:14:28.537 --> 00:14:33.697
saw Tier 0 wait for Tier 1, saw the apply
refuse until a person confirmed the gate,

212
00:14:33.697 --> 00:14:38.777
and then applied fifty-one requests with
no differences left on the read-back.

213
00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:44.590
Carry one thing forward: eligibility for
each role goes to the role's P R V group.

214
00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:49.327
Next is Module 5: PIM for
Groups and the P R V groups.

215
00:14:49.327 --> 00:14:54.458
A P R V group's own state has to be
governed too: who is a member or owner,

216
00:14:54.458 --> 00:14:59.028
until when, on which ticket,
and how membership is activated.

217
00:14:59.028 --> 00:15:02.206
In Module 5 you keep that
state in one reviewed file,

218
00:15:02.206 --> 00:15:06.350
and see the guards that keep people
from losing access by mistake.
